title: Reading the log costs the window
tags: [[Event log]]
kind: rationale
audience: dev
summary: A bounded read of the log reads what it returns — not the log behind it — and a filter widens the walk rather than returning nothing.

"Loading the log takes a long time" (user report, September 2026). Reproduced by grafting
200 000 revisions into a bench repository, it had two independent causes:

* `GET /log` built its table of timestamps from ''every'' revision, even for a window of fifty operations — 330–500 ms on 200 000 revisions. It now reads only the revisions of the operations it returns: 15 ms.
* `mf log list`, which shows twenty ''revisions'', sent the daemon no bound and cut on its side — seven seconds in the daemon and about two minutes formatting. Hence the `revisions=N` parameter: 14 ms.

Then a reconcile: one revision of 100 000 operations made twenty revisions 100 000
operations again — 0.7 s and 395 MiB for twenty lines. So `revisions` and `limit` are
two ''maxima'' (the user's call, replacing "revisions are always whole"): the walk stops
at whichever it reaches first, and the one revision it may cut, the oldest, says so
(`partial`, `op_count`).

''A bound bounds what is returned, not the search.'' With a filter (`metarecord_uuid`,
`since`, `until`), the walk widens geometrically until it has what was asked for or
reaches the root: `--limit 10` on one old metarecord used to return nothing, because
none of the repository's last ten operations happened to be its.

What stays proportional to the log, by construction: `mode=tree` (a graph needs every
branch). The totals are B-tree statistics now, not counts. The read side was moved out
of the routes into `log_view.rs` so its cost could be tested on its own:
`tests/log_cost.rs` holds a fifty-operation window to the same number of keys on a log of
five thousand and of five hundred thousand ([[Storage]]).
